            "name": "Davis, Devin Phillip"
          }
        ],
        "description": "<p>Preregistered 27-qubit stability benchmark on the IBM Heron r2 processor <code>ibm_fez</code> (protocol <code>OSIRIS-WP2-27Q-FEZ</code>, job <code>dc1tqvinj2vs73dfrts0</code>; 48 ISA circuits, 98,304 raw shots, 28 s QPU, constant 52-CZ / 378-SX heavy-hex tree with zero routing SWAPs) paired with multi-width SIMULATED hypercube benchmarks up to 96 qubits and hardware re-scoring of archived Heron r2 datasets.</p><ul><li>Resolves both the refuted legacy Eq. (7) marginal-product self-cost artifact (0.50918 at Q = 27) and the Weed&ndash;Bach O(S^{-1/Q}) finite-shot sparsity artifact of raw plug-in Kantorovich optimal transport via exact multivariate-hypergeometric sample-size-matched two-by-two split-sample Kantorovich and Sinkhorn (epsilon = 0.05) U-statistics.</li><li>Across theta in {2.118, 2.618, 3.118} rad on <code>ibm_fez</code>, matched split-sample Sinkhorn collapses inter-block A/A drift to &le; 0.000076 (&lt; 0.005 threshold, H1 supported), confirms no special stability advantage at the golden-ratio angle theta* = 2.618 rad (H2 supported), and detects controlled coherent perturbations Delta-theta = 0.45 rad monotonically at 13.76 sigma (H3 supported).</li><li>On SIMULATED Uniform({0,1}^96), raw plug-in LP suffers a +855.48 sigma false-drift artifact whereas matched split-sample Sinkhorn yields z = -0.34 sigma; archived <code>ibm_marrakesh</code> (job <code>dau0q3qhcrkc73durtgg</code>) and 49-bit <code>OSIRIS-QEC-001</code> (job <code>dbtl2vb2kf8s73c9a0d0</code>) counts corroborate clean separation of hardware drift from sampling sparsity.</li></ul>",
        "keywords": [
          "optimal transport",
          "Sinkhorn divergence",
          "finite-sample debiasing",
          "quantum benchmarking",
          "IBM Heron r2",
          "ibm_fez",
          "preregistration",
          "OSIRIS-WP2-27Q-FEZ"
        ],
